dma-fence: fence context numbers of two kexts do not collide

Audit finding X3. Each DRM kext links its own copy of dma-fence.c and each copy counted
contexts from 1, while fences cross between the copies (a display out-fence waited on by
the GPU scheduler, a GPU fence in a buffer the display scans out). "Same context" means
"same timeline, the later one replaces the earlier" in the scheduler's dependency
handling, in dma_resv_add_fence() and in the fence merge: a display fence and a GPU fence
with the same small context number were taken for one timeline and one of the waits was
dropped. Each copy now numbers its contexts from a base made of the address of its own
counter, which differs between kexts of one kernel collection.

--- a/drivers/dma-buf/dma-fence.c
+++ b/drivers/dma-buf/dma-fence.c
@@ -86,6 +86,14 @@
 static atomic64_t dma_fence_context_counter = ATOMIC64_INIT(1);
 
 /*
+ * SiliconForest: every kext that links this file has its own counter, and fences
+ * cross between kexts. The low 40 bits of the counter's own address, above 24
+ * bits of count, keep the numbers of two copies apart.
+ */
+#define	SF_DMA_FENCE_CONTEXT_BASE \
+	((u64)((uintptr_t)&dma_fence_context_counter & 0xffffffffffULL) << 24)
+
+/*
  * allocate an array of fence contexts
  */
 u64
@@ -92,7 +100,8 @@
 dma_fence_context_alloc(unsigned num)
 {
 
-	return (atomic64_fetch_add(num, &dma_fence_context_counter));
+	return (SF_DMA_FENCE_CONTEXT_BASE +
+	    atomic64_fetch_add(num, &dma_fence_context_counter));
 }
 
